"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,713\nFrom fragile to stable\n\n4\n00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:18,667\nIn nineteen hundred, two German psychologists had people learn a list of nonsense syllable pairs,\n\n5\n00:00:18,816 --> 00:00:19,917\nthen a second list.\n\n6\n00:00:20,416 --> 00:00:22,246\nWhen the second list came right away,\n\n7\n00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:23,975\nmemory for the first suffered.\n\n8\n00:00:24,443 --> 00:00:26,104\nWhen it came six minutes later,\n\n9\n00:00:26,187 --> 00:00:27,802\nthe first list held up better.\n\n10\n00:00:28,214 --> 00:00:29,955\nNew memories, they concluded,\n\n11\n00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:31,241\nneed time to set.\n\n12\n00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:33,885\nThey called the process consolidation.\n\n13\n00:00:34,055 --> 00:00:35,486\nAt the level of synapses,\n\n14\n00:00:35,538 --> 00:00:37,441\nconsolidation has a signature.\n\n15\n00:00:37,934 --> 00:00:39,682\nIn the early nineteen seventies,\n\n16\n00:00:39,819 --> 00:00:43,883\nresearchers gave a pathway into the hippocampus a brief burst of stimulation,\n\n17\n00:00:44,195 --> 00:00:46,643\nand the connection stayed stronger for hours,\n\n18\n00:00:46,821 --> 00:00:48,151\nand in later work,\n\n19\n00:00:48,171 --> 00:00:48,405\nfor days.\n\n20\n00:00:48,812 --> 00:00:51,641\nMaking that change last requires new proteins.\n\n21\n00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:57,386\nIn animals, blocking protein synthesis around learning leaves short term memory intact,\n\n22\n00:00:57,486 --> 00:00:59,422\nbut long term memory fails.\n\n23\n00:00:59,804 --> 00:01:01,270\nThe window is hours,\n\n24\n00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:02,184\nnot weeks.\n\n25\n00:01:02,354 --> 00:01:03,869\nWhere is the memory itself?\n\n26\n00:01:04,316 --> 00:01:11,233\nIn mice, researchers tagged the small set of hippocampal cells that were active while an animal learned to fear a place.\n\n27\n00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:13,535\nLater, in a completely different box,\n\n28\n00:01:13,555 --> 00:01:15,855\nthey switched those cells on with light.\n\n29\n00:01:16,344 --> 00:01:18,570\nThe mice froze, as if remembering.\n\n30\n00:01:18,924 --> 00:01:21,989\nA sparse set of cells was enough to bring the memory back.\n\n31\n00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:26,299\nThis is an engram: a physical trace of an experience.\n\n32\n00:01:26,469 --> 00:01:29,635\nOver longer spans, memories reorganize.\n\n33\n00:01:29,999 --> 00:01:32,854\nThe hippocampus helps bind an experience together,\n\n34\n00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:36,344\nand gradually the cortex takes on more of the storage.\n\n35\n00:01:36,772 --> 00:01:40,575\nThat is why hippocampal damage tends to spare old memories,\n\n36\n00:01:40,771 --> 00:01:42,637\nand hit recent ones hardest.\n\n37\n00:01:43,112 --> 00:01:45,019\nAnd as the last episode showed,\n\n38\n00:01:45,224 --> 00:01:48,778\na strong schema can make this hand off happen much faster.\n\n39\n00:01:48,948 --> 00:01:50,118\nTwo cautions.\n\n40\n00:01:50,443 --> 00:01:52,314\nThe engram work is in mice,\n\n41\n00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:54,109\nmostly with fear memories,\n\n42\n00:01:54,283 --> 00:01:56,073\na long way from a lecture hall.\n\n43\n00:01:56,383 --> 00:01:59,759\nAnd the protein story has exceptions and live debates.\n\n44\n00:02:00,126 --> 00:02:01,699\nWhat is solid is the shape: new memories start fragile,\n\n45\n00:02:02,048 --> 00:02:05,426\nand stabilize with time.\n\n46\n00:02:05,596 --> 00:02:06,361\nFor teaching.\n\n47\n00:02:06,655 --> 00:02:08,550\nTreat new learning as fragile.\n\n48\n00:02:08,932 --> 00:02:13,041\nAvoid piling a second demanding topic straight on top of the first.\n\n49\n00:02:13,475 --> 00:02:14,647\nGive a short pause,\n\n50\n00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:18,301\na summary, or a low stakes question before moving on.\n\n51\n00:02:18,794 --> 00:02:20,517\nCome back within a day or two,\n\n52\n00:02:20,543 --> 00:02:22,202\nwhile the trace is still settling.\n\n53\n00:02:22,563 --> 00:02:25,688\nAnd connect new material to what students already know,\n\n54\n00:02:25,816 --> 00:02:28,618\nso it consolidates into an existing frame.\n\n55\n00:02:28,788 --> 00:02:31,505\nA memory is not finished when the lesson ends.\n\n56\n00:02:31,772 --> 00:02:33,231\nIt is still being built.\n\n"
